    "slug": "somalia-s-auditor-general-hands-over-to-staff-committee-amid-constitutional-standoff",
    "summary": "The Office of the Auditor General, a constitutionally independent body, has transferred responsibilities to a staff committee. The move follows political pressure on the institution.",
    "content": "The Office of the Auditor General of Somalia has handed over its duties to a staff committee, according to a report by Radio Dalsan. The office, established under Article 177 of the Somali Provisional Constitution, is designated as an independent body with administrative and financial autonomy. Its powers and responsibilities are further defined by the Public Financial Management Act (Law No. 17 of 2019) and the Auditor General Act (Law No. 14 of 2023). The handover to a staff committee marks a significant development in the institution's operations.\n\nThe Auditor General's Office is the highest authority responsible for auditing, registering, and safeguarding public assets and financial transactions of the government. Its constitutional independence is intended to ensure accountability and transparency in the management of public funds. The decision to transfer duties to a staff committee comes amid ongoing tensions over the leadership and independence of the office. While details remain limited, the move suggests an internal or external impasse affecting the institution's normal functioning.\n\nThe Somali diaspora has a keen interest in the integrity of public financial management, as many diaspora members contribute remittances and invest in the country. A weakened Auditor General's Office could undermine efforts to combat corruption and ensure that public resources are used effectively. The diaspora has consistently advocated for transparency and accountability, viewing strong oversight institutions as crucial for Somalia's stability and development.\n\nThis development raises concerns about the rule of law and the protection of constitutional bodies in Somalia. The international community, including Somalia's partners, has emphasized the importance of independent oversight institutions for good governance. The handover to a staff committee may be a temporary measure, but it highlights the challenges facing Somalia's democratic institutions. The diaspora will be watching closely to see how this situation evolves and whether the office can maintain its independence.\n\nAttribution: Read the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/23/hantidhowrka-guud-oo-guddiga-shaqaalaha-u-digay/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=hantidhowrka-guud-oo-guddiga-shaqaalaha-u-digay",
